AB  - Electroencephalography (EEG) is a non-invasive test used to determine human brain activity by measuring the signals generated; it can also be used to detect epileptic seizures. Epilepsy, seizure, sleep disturbance, sleepiness, head injury, headache, and brain tumor diagnosis all benefit from this technique. It's also useful for making if a person has truly died from brain damage. Epilepsy is a neurological condition brought on by electrical disruptions. Epilepsy manifests itself through episodes of brief amnesia, unconsciousness, and jerking movements. Adaptations in behavior influence neuronal activity in the brain. Some of our bodily parts can be directly impacted by alterations in neuronal activity. Brain processes including detecting, seeing, and remembering are all carried out in line with the passage of electrical signals. Seizures manifest when the nerve cells are compromised. Both generalized and focal seizures exist. This study proposes that features with the highest class separability can be used for supervised learning tasks to better pick subsets of features from multichannel EEG recordings. In this study, we test the following proposition: Features that are substantially linked with the class labels but not correlated with each other make for an excellent feature subset.
