AU  - H. Abdullah
AU  - S. Z. A. Jalil
T2  - 2025 IEEE 11th International Conference on Smart Instrumentation, Measurement and Applications (ICSIMA)
JO  - 2025 IEEE 11th International Conference on Smart Instrumentation, Measurement and Applications (ICSIMA)
PY  - 2025
SP  - 205
EP  - 210
AB  - Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA) is a prevalent sleep disorder marked by repeated interruptions in breathing, leading to disrupted brain and cardiovascular regulation. While EEG and ECG signals are routinely used in polysomnography, their interaction specifically brain and heart coupling remains underutilized in automated diagnostic systems. This study introduces a novel, interpretable Neurocardiac Coupling Index (NCI) that quantifies bidirectional interactions between EEG and HRV signals across matched frequency bands. Using data from the DREAMS Apnea Database, EEG and ECG signals were processed to extract spectral features and compute directional coupling components: Brain-to-Heart Coupling (BHC) and Heart-to-Brain Coupling (HBC). The final NCI was derived as a normalized, weighted combination of these features. Statistical analysis revealed significantly elevated NCI values in the OSA group compared to controls (p = 0.006), with stronger BHC evident in θ-LF and α-HF pairs. These results suggest enhanced cortical modulation of autonomic activity in OSA, supporting the potential of NCI as a signal-level, non-invasive marker for sleep apnea screening.
