AB  - Refinements in technology have enabled the use of various machine learning methods to improve the evaluation of disorders of consciousness (DOC) patients. This study investigates the application of multiple regression models for consciousness assessment of DOC patients based on EEG features extracted across different frequency bands. Absolute power, relative power, and symbolic transfer entropy are the features considered in the study. The paper implements five regression models: linear regression model, elastic net, support vector regressor, XGBoost, and random forest. Among the models, XGBoost demonstrated the highest predictive accuracy with the highest coefficient of de-termination: 0.855, and the lowest mean square error: 0.111, and the mean absolute error: 0.114. The study highlights XGBoost's effectiveness in assessing consciousness through EEG features, providing a promising tool to improve clinical evaluations and treatment strategies for patients with impaired consciousness.
