AB  - Recently, there has been a lot of interest in the computational electroencephalogram (EEG) to see whether its properties may be used as novel predictors for the vaticination of recovery in the identification of mild brain damage. This composition proposes a computer-backed method for automated brain impairment identification by extracting information from electroencephalogram (EEG) data in order to solve this problem. Power Spectral Density Difference (PSDD), a novel connection metric, is presented. It incorporates a recursive Cosine function (CPSDD). Therefore, developing a method for rigorously flagging and rooting clean EEG data is essential in order to recover high-quality discrimination features using PCA for In addition to feature selection, the method uses a machine learning methodology to classify brain-injured instances into three categories: heavy, moderate, and neutral. Our suggested method for using a machine learning algorithm to assess vaticination state and high delicacy. In the web application, we will eventually be able to describe brain stages as heavy, moderate, and normal.
