AB  - This study aimed to address the efficiency bottleneck of traditional mindfulness meditation training by validating a closed-loop Brain-Computer Interface (BCI) and investigating its intervention effects on novices. Seventeen meditation novices were randomly assigned to either an experimental group ($\mathbf{n}=\mathbf{9}$) that received a $\mathbf{7}$-day, daily $\mathbf{2 0}$-minute BCI closed-loop training, where the system dynamically modulated virtual environment immersiveness based on real-time decoding of prefrontal EEG permutation entropy to provide neurofeedback, or a control group $(n=8)$ that trained in an identical but static environment. A multi-dimensional assessment revealed the following: On the behavioral level, the experimental group demonstrated a significant increase in Mindful Attention Awareness Scale (MAAS) scores (within-group: $\mathbf{t}(\mathbf{8})=\mathbf{2. 9 8}, \mathbf{p}=\mathbf{0. 0 0 1 8}$; between-group post-test: $\mathbf{t}(\mathbf{1 5})=\mathbf{3. 0 3}, \mathrm{p}=0.008$). At the neurophysiological level, the experimental group exhibited a significant post-intervention decrease in the frontal Theta/Alpha power ratio $(\mathrm{t}(8)=-3.62, \mathrm{p}=0.007)$, while the control group showed no such decline. Moreover, the experimental group's meditation scores displayed a stable upward trend (slope $=0.803$), in contrast to the control group's flat trend (slope $=0.073)$. These findings provide convergent evidence from subjective behavior, objective neurophysiology, and dynamic process analysis, confirming that the closed-loop BCI-based training effectively enhances mindfulness efficiency in novices, thereby offering a reliable technical pathway and empirical basis for overcoming the limitations of traditional mindfulness meditation training.
