AB  - This study investigates the efficacy of EEG signal-based spectral and complexity features for emotion detection through a one-dimensional Convolutional Neural Network (1D CNN) architecture. Two complementary feature sets were extracted from EEG recordings: Power Spectral Density (PSD), reflecting frequency-domain energy distribution and Sample Entropy, representing temporal signal complexity. The model classified emotional states along the valence and arousal dimensions. Experimental analysis demonstrated that PSD features achieved higher accuracy 76.85% for valence and 74.67% for arousal compared with 68.78% and 66.82% obtained using Sample Entropy. To verify the statistical significance of these differences, an independent-samples t-test was applied on accuracies from 10-fold cross-validation. The results showed significant improvements in PSD performance for both emotional dimensions: t (13.71) = 6.95, p = 7.61×10-6 for valence and t (15.69) = 4.75, p = 2.30×10-4 for arousal. These findings confirm that PSD-based spectral features capture more stable and discriminative neural representations than entropy-based measures, establishing a statistically validated and computationally efficient 1D-CNN framework for EEG-driven emotion recognition and affect-adaptive human–computer interaction.
