AB  - Electroencephalography(EEG) signals are important indicators of brain activity and states of mind. This study conducts an in-depth exploration of the Healthy Brain Network EEG Dataset, presenting key findings and insights derived from the analysis. The significant features extracted are mean, standard deviation, variance, power spectral densities (Delta, Theta, Alpha, Beta, and Gamma), entropy, wavelet energy, and the Hurst exponent. Early results indicate that Beta and Gamma power components of greater frequency have high correlations with cognitive processing, whereas lower frequency bands like Delta and Theta are associated with relaxation and sleep. Entropy and wavelet energy also play a part in discriminating between active and passive brain states. Advanced machine learning methods are employed to classify common neural patterns associated with different states of mind. The proposed methodology shows promising real-time EEG-based classification potential and opens the doors to applications within neurological diagnostics, brain-computer interfaces, and cognitive research. The research demonstrates significant improvements in EEG-based cognitive state classification by integrating multiple feature extraction techniques, outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods.
