AB  - An estimated 65 million people worldwide suffer from epilepsy, a neurological disorder brought on by aberrant electrical activity in the brain. Large amounts of data are required to train machine learning algorithms, enabling the creation of seizure prediction systems that are accurate and have low false alarm rates. The dataset for this study is the EEG scalp signals obtained from 14 epileptic patients at the University of Siena's Neurology Unit in Italy. With an emphasis on identifying synchronization patterns in EEG signals, a patient-specific seizure prediction method is proposed and assessed. The objective is to determine whether non-invasive EEG data can be utilized to create a reliable seizure prediction, monitoring, and control tool. The prediction framework employs a straightforward, computationally efficient threshold-based classifier and uses synchronization metrics calculated across all pairs of EEG channels. Four distinct classification techniques are evaluated for seizure detection and prediction based on the synchronization between preictal and ictal states.
