AB  - Sleep stage classification plays an important role in the diagnosis of sleep disorder and sleep quality assessment. Most of the previous automatic sleep stage classification methods belong to the supervised learning, focusing exploiting fully labeled data sets. However, it is usually easier to collect data than giving the corresponding labels. In this study, we described a semi-supervised end-to-end automatic sleep staging method based on pseudo-label exploiting raw single-channel electroencephalograms (EEG) which is partial labeled. The proposed method involves the construction of deep learning model from partial labeled data. The unlabeled EEG is picked up class as pseudo-label which has the maximum predicted probability and trained with labeled data simultaneously. The results showed that our model achieved similar overall average accuracy (76%-80%) and Cohen’s Kappa coefficient (0.6550.716) to the supervised learning methods (accuracy: 80%82%, Cohen’s Kappa: 0.698-0.743) on Sleep-EDFx dataset. This demonstrated that the proposed method could effectively extract features from raw EEG signals and achieve similar or even better results compared to the supervised learning methods.
