AB  - This work provides a novel framework for identifying coma and brain death consciousness states by analysing frequency power and phase synchrony features from electroencephalogram (EEG). The proposed analysis of pairs of EEG electrodes using complex extensions of Empirical Mode Decomposition (EMD) permits the extraction of information related to the state of the brain function. Analysis on 34 subjects in the coma and quasi-brain-death states suggests that phase synchrony constitutes a feasible feature to discriminate quasi-brain-death from coma state. Thus, illustrate the effectiveness of the proposed methods for brain consciousness identification. The predictive power of the features extracted is evaluated by building classification models using support vector machine (SVM) and evaluation the models through receiver operating characteristic (ROC) analysis.
