AB  - Traditional EEG-based anxiety detection struggles with generalization across subjects due to individual variability. A key bottleneck is the "manifold entanglement" between anxiety patterns and subject-specific physiology, where conventional domain adaptation often causes "over-pruning" of key anxiety semantics. To address this, we propose DREAM, a meta-learning-based dynamic reconstruction model. First, we introduce a Two-Stage Indirect Adversarial Disentangled Decomposition (TSIAD) strategy. Using a "Siphon-then-Purge" mechanism with Gradient Reversal Layers, it truncates domain information flow at the source, purifying features without compromising anxiety structure. Furthermore, we incorporate Multi-Scenario Statistical Feature Mixing (MSFIM) and Cross-Attention for robust few-shot adaptation. Experiments on the SWU-ADEM dataset (76 valid subjects) show that DREAM achieves state-of-the-art accuracy of 94.09% in subject-independent settings, successfully overcoming manifold entanglement for reliable anxiety screening.
