AB  - Biomedical signals such as EEG and ECG provide critical insights into human physiological conditions. Accurate analysis of these signals facilitates early diagnosis of sleep disorders and cardiac arrhythmias. However, existing methods often face challenges in feature integration and preprocessing. This study proposes a hybrid machine learning approach for the classification of EEG sleep stages and ECG arrhythmias. EEG data from the Sleep-EDF Expanded Database and ECG data from the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database were preprocessed using noise removal and normalization techniques. Time-domain, frequency-domain, and chaos-based features were extracted and combined into comprehensive feature vectors. The framework achieved an overall accuracy of 95% for both tasks. Precision and F1-scores were 0.98 and 0.97 for EEG, and 0.95 for ECG. Multi-domain features significantly enhanced discriminative power, demonstrating that the proposed framework is accurate, robust, and interpretable for automated biomedical signal analysis.
