AB  - Electroencephalography (EEG) is among the essential tools for the analysis of brain dynamics and the diagnosis of neurological disorders such as epilepsy, sleep disturbances, and cognitive dysfunctions, but its use poses a big problem because of the difficulty of its treatment. The challenge of EEG signal processing is great due to its low amplitude, high non-stationarity, and high sensitivity to physiological and environmental artifacts. The signal quality is impacted by these disturbances, which also compromises the reliability of the extracted characteristics“ including temporal, spectral, nonlinear, and connectivity metrics”. The influence of common EEG artifacts is examined in this article, such as ocular movements (EOG), muscle activity (EMG), cardiac interference (ECG), electrode motion, and power-line noise, on both the neurophysiological information contained in EEG recordings and the derived quantitative features. We will review conventional and advanced filtering methods, including FIR and IIR filters, notch and band-pass filtering, Independent Component Analysis (ICA), Empirical Mode Decomposition (EMD), wavelet-based denoising, and Singular Spectrum Analysis (SSA). We present a methodological framework to select the best filtering strategies based on the application domain “epileptic seizure detection, sleep scoring, brain-computer interfaces (BCI), or deep-learning-based classification ”. The results indicate that a rigorous preprocessing pipeline is required to guarantee signal integrity and improve the strength of analytical results and machine-learning models used in clinical neurophysiology.
