AB  - This case report presents a two-month neurofeedback (NF) intervention for a male patient in his 50 s with post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) and depression. Training at $\mathbf{C z}$ reinforced low-beta $(\mathbf{1 5}-\mathbf{1 8 ~ H z})$ while inhibiting 28 Hz and $22-36 \mathrm{~Hz}$. EEG analysis using the Maximum Entropy Method revealed a transient delta rebound after the sixth session, coinciding with temporary distress but followed by stabilization. Alpha power under eyes-closed conditions showed a sustained increase, associated with the disappearance of trauma-related nightmares and improved sleep. Notably, the rewarded low-beta band itself did not increase, suggesting that clinical improvements stemmed from network-level reorganization rather than isolated reinforcement. Subjective reports indicated reduced irritability, enhanced emotional regulation, and improved self-reflection, corroborated by clinical observation. These findings highlight delta rebound as a potential neurophysiological marker and demonstrate the therapeutic promise of low-beta neurofeedback in PTSD.
