AB  - Circadian rhythms are involved in many human behaviors and biological signals. The critical role of circadian rhythm in maintaining homeostasis is well established, in which disturbances in circadian rhythms affect health. Various methods using markers of body movement can indirectly detect circadian rhythms. Direct capture of these rhythms requires use of the electroencephalogram (EEG) method. Recently, studies on circadian rhythm have used Higuchi’s fractal dimension (FD) to indicate the degree of complexity of brain activity. Our recent study showed that circadian rhythms reflect different temporal-scale complexities. In this context, we hypothesized that the relevant range of parameters focusing on temporal-scale dependency in Higuchi’s FD might improve the detection accuracy of circadian rhythms. To prove this hypothesis, we evaluated the optimized parameters to maximize the difference in FD during the day.
