AB  - Micro-expression recognition (MER) via Electroencephalogram (EEG) signals offers a more objective physiological perspective compared to traditional image-based methods. However, verifying the effectiveness of modeling heterogeneous relationships between local neural activities and global dependencies remains a crucial task. This paper investigates the Effectiveness of a Dual-Branch Architecture using a novel Deep Information Extraction and Fusion Network (DIEAFN) for 6-class MER tasks. DIEAFN is designed with a specific dual-branch structure: a Multi-level Convolutional Attention (MLCA) module for capturing fine-grained local brain patterns, and a Global State Space Duality (GSSD) module to model long-range dynamic interactions across EEG electrodes. An Adaptive Cross-Attention Fusion (ACAF) module is integrated to synergize these representations. To validate the rationality of this design, we visualize the Effective Receptive Field (ERF), which confirms that the model successfully integrates localized neural oscillations with global brain network dynamics as intended. Experimental evaluations on the SWU-ME-I dataset show an accuracy of 82.23% with Differential Entropy (DE) features and 94.09% with Power Spectral Density (PSD) features, further supporting the effectiveness of the proposed architecture in classifying complex micro-expressions.
