AB  - This paper proposes a novel Adaptive Phase Space Reconstruction-Diffusion Maps-Gaussian Process Regression (APSR-DM-GPR) framework for multi-channel EEG signal prediction. Traditional EEG prediction methods often struggle with the nonlinear, non-stationary characteristics of neural signals and fail to effectively utilize spatiotemporal correlations across multiple channels. To address these limitations, our framework integrates nonlinear dynamics theory with modern machine learning techniques through three core components: adaptive parameter estimation for phase space reconstruction, diffusion maps for nonlinear dimensionality reduction, and Gaussian process regression for probabilistic prediction. Experimental results on a 64-channel EEG dataset demonstrate the framework's exceptional performance, achieving an average coefficient of determination ($R^{2}$) of 0.9909, significantly outperforming LSTM (0.9396) and ARIMA (0.8896) models. The proposed method exhibits strong robustness, multi-channel synergy, and interpretability, making it suitable for clinical applications including epileptic seizure prediction, sleep stage analysis, brain-computer interfaces, and neurodegenerative disease monitoring. The standardized design ensures easy extension to other spatiotemporal sequence prediction tasks, offering a new technical pathway for neural signal processing research.
