TI  - Research on Emotion Recognition Method Based on EEG Signals Using Improved CNN-LSTM
AU  - L. Gao
T2  - 2026 5th International Symposium on Computer Applications and Information Technology (ISCAIT)
JO  - 2026 5th International Symposium on Computer Applications and Information Technology (ISCAIT)
PY  - 2026
SP  - 853
EP  - 857
AB  - Electroencephalogram (EEG) signals, as physiological signals reflecting human brain activities, have unique objectivity and reliability in the field of emotion recognition. Aiming at the problems of insufficient feature extraction, gradient disappearance and weak model generalization ability of the traditional Convolutional Neural Network-Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM) in EEG signal emotion recognition, an improved CNN-LSTM emotion recognition method based on attention mechanism and residual connection is proposed. Firstly, the original EEG signals are preprocessed, and noise interference is removed and effective EEG components are separated through Detrended Fluctuation Analysis (DFA) and Independent Component Analysis (ICA). Secondly, a CNN module with residual blocks is constructed to enhance the spatial feature extraction ability and alleviate gradient disappearance. Then, Bidirectional LSTM (Bi-LSTM) is introduced to capture the temporal dependence of EEG signals, and the channel attention mechanism is combined to adaptively enhance the weight of key channel features. Finally, the emotional category classification is realized through the fully connected layer. Experiments are carried out based on the public DEAP dataset, and four types of emotions in the valence-arousal two-dimensional emotion model (high valence-high arousal, high valence-low arousal, low valencehigh arousal, low valence-low arousal) are selected for verification. The results show that the average recognition accuracy of the proposed method reaches 89.7%, which is 7.2%, 11.5% and 13.3% higher than that of the traditional CNN-LSTM, single CNN and single LSTM models respectively, and it shows good generalization performance on different subject groups. This research provides a more efficient solution for EEG signal emotion recognition, which can be widely applied in human-computer interaction, auxiliary diagnosis of emotional disorders and other fields.
