AB  - Emotion recognition from electroencephalogram (EEG) is key in developing affective brain-computer interfaces (BCIs). The functional interaction of brain networks encodes emotion. Using functional EEG network connectivity for emotion recognition is promising but remains difficult because of three challenges. First, EEG network connectivity is typically quantified by symmetric positive definite matrices represented on Riemannian manifolds, making existing Euclidean recognition methods not directly applicable. Second, the large EEG channel number leads to high-dimensional and noisy connectivity matrices that are difficult to classify. Third, aggregating EEG phase and spectral information on Riemannian manifolds remains elusive. Here, we develop a new Riemannian matrix discriminant analysis (RMDA) method for emotion recognition from high-dimensional EEG covariance, coherence, and crosspower spectrum density matrices. RMDA projects connectivity matrices onto a low-dimensional Riemannian manifold and uses k-nearest neighbor classification on the low-dimensional manifold for emotion recognition. RMDA further extends to the complex Fourier domain to incorporate phase information and uses Bayesian-based frequency fusion to aggregate spectral information.We show that RMDA achieves state-of-the-art recognition results on three representative EEG-emotion datasets. For subject-dependent emotion recognition, we achieve: 1) 94.37% on the SEED dataset; 2) 63.83% for valance and 64.77% for arousal on the DEAP dataset; 3) 65.00% for valance and 62.88% for arousal on the MAHNOB-HCI dataset; For subject-independent emotion recognition, we achieve: 1) 85.19% on the SEED dataset; 2) 67.11% for valance and 66.41% for arousal on the DEAP dataset; 3) 64.81% for valance and 65.20% for arousal on the MAHNOB-HCI dataset. Meanwhile, our RMDA is parameter-efficient compared to current methods. Last, our results reveal the importance of frontal connectivity in emotion recognition, which holds promise to effectively use brain network connectivity for emotion recognition in affective BCIs. The source code can be found at https://github.com/lalalaGegrx/Riemannian-Matrix-Discriminant-Analysis/tree/master.
