AB  - Brain computer interface (BCI) is a special means of human-computer interaction. Since the 1970s, after nearly half a century of development, it has been used in many fields such as medical care. In recent years, imaginary speech has emerged as a new neural paradigm based on internal arousal of brain activity for natural communication without the need for auditory language. Since no features suitable for imaginary speech have been found, many studies are underway, including traditional common spatial pattern (CSP) and support vector machine (SVM) methods, as well as convolutional neural networks (CNN) and long short-term memory (LSTM) and other deep learning methods. In this paper, instantaneous frequency and power spectral entropy are used as the characteristics of imaginary speech EEG signals. On this basis, the twin neural network is used to compare the similarity of different categories of imaginary pronunciations for distance learning. Finally, k-nearest neighbor (k-NN) algorithm analyzes the above features and realizes the classification of imaginary speech. The experimental results show that the method can effectively recognize the two-class imaginary speech, and the recognition accuracy is better than the traditional algorithm.
