AB  - Electroencephalogram (EEG) that measures the electrical activity of the brain has been used extensively to recognize emotion. Normally feature based emotion recognition requires a strong effort to design the perfect feature or feature set related to the classification of emotion. To curtail the manual human effort we designed a model by using a virtual image from EEG with Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). Initially, we calculated Pearson's correlation coefficients form different sub-bands of EEG to formulate a virtual image. Later, this virtual image was fed into a CNN architecture to classify emotion. We made two distinct protocols; between these, protocol-1 was to classify positive and negative emotion and protocol-2 was to classify four distinct emotions. An overall maximum accuracy of 81.51% on valence and 79.42% on arousal was obtained by using internationally authorized DEAP dataset. Our proposed method is helpful in recognizing emotions efficiently.
