AB  - Emotion recognition from electroencephalography (EEG) is a key enabling technology for affective computing, brain-computer interfaces, and mental-health monitoring. Signals are high-dimensional, non-stationary, and subject-specific, which complicates robust modeling and interpretability. We propose a hybrid, explainable pipeline that fuses Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) temporal modeling with a Multi-View Graph Transformer (MVGT) that learns adaptive inter-channel and inter-band relations. Per-channel features (Hjorth parameters, skewness, kurtosis, differential entropy) are computed across canonical bands (theta/alpha/beta/gamma). The BiLSTM extracts temporo-sequential embeddings, while MVGT computes spatial-spectral node embeddings; fused representations are classified for valence and arousal. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) are used post hoc to produce channel-level and band-level attributions. Evaluation on the DEAP dataset shows BiLSTM baseline accuracy of 82.35% and MVGT-BiLSTM fused accuracy of 93%, with SHAP maps aligned to known neurophysiology. Detailed derivations, symbol definitions, and implementation details are provided to enable reproducibility.
