AB  - EEG-based emotion recognition faces significant challenges due to the non-stationary nature of EEG signals, inter-subject variability, and the distributed representation of emotional information across spatial, spectral, and temporal domains. To address these issues, this paper proposes a Hierarchical Multi-Domain Attention Network (HMDA-Net). The framework constructs spatial–frequency representations using differential entropy features and employs hierarchical multi-scale convolution to enhance spatial–spectral modeling. A parallel spatial–channel attention mechanism is introduced to refine discriminative features, while a Transformer-based temporal module captures global dynamic dependencies. Extensive experiments conducted on the DEAP and SEED datasets demonstrate that HMDA-Net achieves 97.61% and 97.12% accuracy for Valence and Arousal classification on DEAP, and 96.17% accuracy on SEED, outperforming representative baseline models. Ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of the multi-scale and multi-domain attention design. The proposed method provides an effective solution for robust EEG-based emotion recognition.
