AB  - EEG-based emotion recognition faces critical challenges regarding subject-independent generalization and interpretability, particularly with consumer-grade wearables. Existing deep learning models often prioritize accuracy over mechanistic understanding or cross-subject stability. This paper proposes a novel Energy Landscape Analysis (ELA) framework to decode emotional states. By modeling binarized neural activity with a pairwise Maximum Entropy Model (pMEM), we map brain dynamics onto an energy landscape in which emotional states act as metastable attractors. We extract interpretable features characterizing stability (basin depth), complexity (barriers), and volatility. Evaluating this framework across three public datasets (DREAMER, AMIGOS, GAMEEMO) using a consistent 14-channel Emotiv EPOC montage, we find that ELA features support robust Subject-Independent (SI) classification, particularly for Valence (86.8%), significantly outperforming Subject-Dependent (SD) baselines. While Arousal generalization proves sensitive to domain shifts between passive and active tasks, interpretability analysis reveals a consistent dynamical signature: high-intensity emotions are associated with deeper, more stable attractor basins and reduced transition flickering. These findings establish ELA as a promising "white-box" framework that balances predictive performance with theoretical insight.
