AB  - Managing cognitive load is central to designing adaptive human-computer interaction - particularly in contexts where personalized learning must respond to individual user needs, or where system reliability is nonnegotiable in safety-critical environment. This paper presents a real-time multimodal deep-learn configuration that attracts EEG, peripheral physiological signals and gaze information to determine the mental workload of someone. Much of the work in the past only focuses on one modality or merges features naively and this is simply all that makes the models weak and fails to take advantage of cross-modality. In the combination of modelling the brain activity, autonomic arousal and visual attention, there is a far better signal of cognitive-load in our system. The architecture can be thought of as dividing the data types into distinct deep subnetworks, namely, an encoder consisting of CNN-BiLSTM (when applied to EEG) and a CNNLSTM combo with a pair of plain fully-connected layers (when applied to the gaze features). These are then combined with cross-modal attention which balances out each modality on the fly rather than simply overlaying them. This will enable the model to concentrate on what is important at a specific time. We tested and trained the network with a publicly available multimodal dataset in which the protocol is subject independent and real-time sliding-window labelling. The experiments demonstrate that trimodal fusion including attention is significant in comparison to unimodal and bi-modal baseline, emphasizing the effectiveness of the offered approach to adaptive interactive systems in reality.
