AB  - EEG-based emotion recognition has shown promise for broad affective states, yet current systems often miss subtle emotional nuances. This study introduces a multilevel analysis framework that classifies both primary emotion categories and their intensity levels by analysing spectral and temporal EEG dynamics. The proposed approach uses hybrid deep learning models-CNN-LSTM, standalone LSTM to jointly capture spatial dependencies and time-varying patterns. The workflow integrates robust preprocessing, frequency-time feature construction, and a hierarchical classification mechanism for detecting fine variations in arousal and valence. Using the DEAP dataset with a subject-adaptive testing protocol, the system shows improved identification of fine-grained emotional differences compared to single-level classifiers [14], [15]. Real-time evaluation with sliding-window inference demonstrates low latency suitable for continuous monitoring. We also discuss deployment considerations, including optimisation strategies, model compression, and personalised calibration. Overall, results indicate that the framework is appropriate for mental health monitoring, responsive human-computer interfaces, and other affective computing applications requiring detailed emotion profiling.
