AB  - In this work, we tackle the limitations of static facial representations in multimodal affective computing by introducing a temporal modeling approach. Specifically, we extract sequential face patch embeddings from uniformly sampled video frames, capturing dynamic facial expressions over time. These visual sequences are integrated with EEG-based differential entropy (DE) features, computed across multiple frequency bands, using a token-level fusion architecture. This framework aligns and jointly encodes temporal patterns from both modalities, enabling the model to capture fine-grained emotional dynamics. To further enhance interpretability, we incorporate token gate modules that learn modality-specific attention across temporal tokens. Experimental results on benchmark datasets demonstrate that our method achieves improved performance in continuous emotion recognition tasks, especially along the valence, arousal, dominance, and liking dimensions. The proposed model not only advances the fusion of temporal multimodal signals but also offers insights into cross-modal temporal saliency, contributing to more robust and interpretable affective computing systems.
