AB  - Mood disorders such as bipolar disorder and major depressive disorder represent persistent challenges in psychiatric medicine due to their episodic nature, diagnostic subjectivity, and biological complexity. This paper introduces AMP-Fuse, a modular, cyber-physical architecture for adaptive multimodal biomarker fusion, aimed at transforming psychiatric diagnostics and real-time intervention. The proposed system integrates a neuro-symbolic reasoning pipeline that dynamically fuses behavioral rhythms (actigraphy), neuroelectrical activity (EEG), and biochemical markers into a unified, context-aware psychiatric interface. At its core, an adaptive inference engine employs attention-guided learning and bidirectional neuro-computational feedback loops to personalize psychiatric decision-making across temporal scales. A human-in-the-Ioop co-adaptation mechanism ensures that algorithmic learning remains clinically aligned and ethically grounded. The resulting output is a real-time Cognitive Digital Twin-a living, interpretable simulation of patient mood dynamics-designed for seamless integration in ambulatory, clinical, and home-based environments. AMP-Fuse lays the foundation for the next generation of precision psychiatry systems: scalable, explainable, and continuously evolving with clinician feedback.
