AB  - With advancements in Deep Learning (DL), emotion recognition using electroencephalograph (EEG) signals in immersive Virtual Reality (VR) environments has demonstrated promising contributions to cognitive training and therapeutic interventions. However, existing models mostly depend on EEG data and traditional classifiers, which limits their robustness and adaptability in real-world settings. Furthermore, struggles arise from poor cross-subject generalization, a lack of temporal consistency, and limited interpretability. Moreover, unimodal pipelines fail to capture the full spectrum of emotional responses, particularly under noisy conditions. In this research, VR-EmotionNet is proposed, which is a multimodal, subject-invariant emotion recognition system that increases recognition accuracy and enables realtime adaptation of VR environments. The VR-EmotionNet combines EEG and heartbeat signals from wearable sensors. Then, the input signals are passed through a Temporal-Difference Minimizing Neural Network (TDMNN), enabling smooth emotional transitions, augmented with the Contrastive Learning method for Inter-Subject Alignment (CLISA) to learn subject-invariant representations. Additionally, a self-attention mechanism is integrated to highlight prominent temporal and spatial features and improve interpretability. Experimental evaluation of VR-EmotionNet improved average recognition accuracy by up to 98.89% and enhanced cross-subject generalization compared to CNN-Transformer and TDMNN baselines, validating the effectiveness of VR-EmotionNet for emotion-aware VR-Internet of Things (IoT) applications.
